<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>THead</h1>
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.THead" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>THead</tt> displays a head element on a page. It displays the content
enclosed in its body and the page title set by the <tt>Title</tt> property.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
The control also renders the stylesheets and scripts that controls registered during the
request. Four methods of <tt>TClientScriptManager</tt> put content there.
</p>
<ul id="u1" class="block-content">
  <li><tt>registerStyleSheet</tt></li>
  <li><tt>registerStyleSheetFile</tt></li>
  <li><tt>registerHeadJavaScript</tt></li>
  <li><tt>registerHeadJavaScriptFile</tt></li>
</ul>
<p class="block-content">
Scripts render only when the client supports JavaScript. This is why a control such as
<a href="?page=Controls.TextHighlighter">TTextHighlighter</a>, which registers a
stylesheet of its own, needs a <tt>THead</tt> on the page to look right.
</p>

<h2>What the head holds</h2>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Renders</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>Title</tt></td>
	<td>The <tt>&lt;title&gt;</tt> element. The control HTML-encodes the value.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>BaseUrl</tt></td>
	<td>A <tt>&lt;base&gt;</tt> element, which sets the URL that relative links on the page resolve against.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>ShortcutIcon</tt></td>
	<td>A <tt>&lt;link rel="shortcut icon"&gt;</tt> element, the icon a browser shows for the page.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>MetaTags</tt></td>
	<td>One <tt>&lt;meta&gt;</tt> element per entry.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
The body content of the control renders last, after everything above.
</p>

<h2>Meta tags</h2>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>MetaTags</tt> is a collection of <tt>TMetaTag</tt> objects. A template adds them as
child tags, and code adds them to the collection at any point before rendering. A meta tag
carries either <tt>Name</tt> or <tt>HttpEquiv</tt> together with <tt>Content</tt>, and
<tt>Charset</tt> renders a character set declaration instead. Its <tt>Scheme</tt> property
is deprecated, because HTML5 dropped the attribute behind it.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:THead&gt;
  &lt;com:TMetaTag HttpEquiv="Pragma" Content="no-cache" /&gt;
  &lt;com:TMetaTag Name="keywords" Content="Prado" /&gt;
&lt;/com:THead&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
Note, <tt>TPage</tt> has a property <tt>Head</tt> that refers to
the <tt>THead</tt> control currently on the page. A page can have at most one <tt>THead</tt>
control. Although not required, it is recommended to place a <tt>THead</tt> on your page.
Without a <tt>THead</tt> on the page, stylesheets and javascripts in the current page
theme will not be rendered.
</p>

</com:TContent>
